@veikkoeeva, you are right.
At first, I wanted to make IMembershipTable and IRemindersTable extend IProvider. Therefore making them standard providers, and delete mentions of **any** specific provider from orleans. That will make the out-of-the-box configuration not as easy, but we can extract this "easy configuration" layer out of orleans and provide some "standard configurations" packages, which internally load everything dynamically but expose an easy interface.
Then we can add an interface ITestableProvider:IProvider that is responsible for creating (and closing) a test environment for the implemented provider. then, for example: we could filter all IMembershipTable that are also ITestableProvider and create the test environment, run all membership tests and close the environment. could work.
This is good, although I'm a bit concerned about throwing baby away with the bath water.

I'm trying to look at this from the 80/20 rule perspective. Would the case of a separate data store for reminders be a 20% case or a 80% case? If it's a 20% (or less) case, then would it be more intuitive to keep the notion of SystemStore that covers liveness, reminders and potentially other system components by default and allow to override reminders with an alternate store?

I'm thinking from pure usability, first time user experience point of view. Wouldn't we degrade the novice experience by requiring them to configure liveness store and reminders store instead of a single system store (by default)?
@sergeybykov I agree, that's why I didn't remove the "DataConnectionString" key from the xml settings, I just added the "AdoInvariant" key. that way, a novice (which IMHO will probably use xml for configuration) will configure Orleans _the same_ as now plus being able to configure the ado invariant. While advanced users will be able to override this default by setting a different reminders provider via the DataConnectionStringForReminders and AdoInvariantForReminders properties. I have thought about just adding an option to override the DataConnectionString only for reminders, but that will make it less straightforward for someone that's working with the GlobalConfiguration class directly. I tried to keep past, present and future users happy.
